package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// Change the caller's own toolsets while it runs: load one, set one aside,
/// and see where each stands. Unstable behind `agent-spawn`.
///
/// It shares admission, the session ceiling and the function budget with the
/// host's model-facing `load_tools` tool, so a harness that imports it can
/// still let its model call `load_tools`.
///
/// A loaded toolset is one more component in the caller's own workload, with
/// its own grants; the caller inherits none of them. The implementer offers
/// its tools through the caller's own `tools` import and dispatches calls to
/// it (so `tool-loading-provider` exports both interfaces), and offers them
/// only to the component that loaded it.
///
/// Admission and the session ceiling are as `spawn-types` states; a load
/// that needs a grantor returns `needs-approval` at once, never holding the
/// run.
///
/// Already held. Before asking a grantor, `load` checks the caller's
/// toolsets for an identical spec (same digest): an `offered` one is
/// returned unchanged; a `ready` one returns to `offered` with no rollout
/// once the session ceiling checks (snapshot, policy, revocation generation)
/// pass again; a `loading` one is joined, and the load resolves with that
/// rollout's result. A different spec under a name already held is
/// `invalid-request`, before any prompt. A toolset the person removed is not
/// held.
///
/// Same-run loading. A load serves the run in progress. Binding a toolset
/// that is not bound yet is a rollout: the workload moves to a revision that
/// adds the toolset's component, attached to the same session. The caller's
/// instance keeps running the current run while the implementer routes calls
/// to the new toolset by its component identity in the new revision; the old
/// revision retires once that run ends. Every new component must start and
/// pass its checks first, or `load` answers `unavailable` and the caller is
/// left as it was (a component that started may have run its start-up
/// code). Before publishing, the rollout checks that the toolset's session
/// ceiling entry or admission still stands, so a toolset the person removed
/// meanwhile never becomes callable and the load answers `denied`.
///
/// Next-run fallback. A host that cannot keep the old revision serving the
/// run in progress while the new one serves calls may make the toolset
/// callable from the caller's next run instead. It says so with
/// `load-result.next-run`, and the toolset stays `loading` until the next
/// run starts on the new revision. A harness that sees it finishes its run
/// rather than polling.
///
/// Rollout deadline. Preparing a rollout (pulling, starting and checking the
/// new components) has the operator's deadline, recommended two minutes.
/// Past it the rollout is abandoned: the toolset leaves `loaded()` and frees
/// its name and place, a joined `load` answers `unavailable`, and an
/// identical `load` starts over. A prepared revision waiting for the next
/// run is kept until that run starts on it or the toolset is removed.
///
/// Waiting. `load` resolves when the toolset is `offered` or when `wait`
/// runs out, whichever is first. `wait` is capped by the implementer's limit
/// (recommended 30 seconds) and by what the caller's run has left; none
/// takes that limit. If the wait runs out first, `load` returns the toolset
/// as `loading`: the rollout continues, `loaded` shows when it is `offered`
/// (or drops it if the rollout fails), and an identical `load` joins it.
///
/// New definitions mid-run. The caller's next `tools.list` includes an
/// `offered` toolset's tools and leaves out a `ready` one's. A harness that
/// loads tools, or lets its model call `load_tools`, calls `tools.list`
/// again before its next model call; the host serves it without running a
/// guest. Where a provider can change tools mid-conversation without
/// rewriting the prompt's prefix, the host's model adapter sends only the
/// difference. Aliases never change within a session.
///
/// Function budget. A model's budget of functions offered at once comes from
/// its model binding (`max-offered-functions`), which the workload manifest
/// may only lower. A caller's budget is the smallest among the model aliases
/// it is granted, for the whole session. When a load would exceed it, the
/// implementer sets aside the least recently called toolset this caller
/// loaded (never called before called, then the oldest load) and reports it
/// in `load-result.set-aside`. It never sets aside a pinned toolset (the
/// operator's and the person's own are pinned) or one loaded or called
/// within the residency window (recommended three runs). When nothing can
/// make room, `load` answers `over-limit` with scope `offered-functions`.
/// Setting aside keeps the component and the approval. Unbinding happens
/// only at the component cap (`loaded-toolsets`), only to set-aside
/// toolsets with no call in flight, and only inside a load's own rollout.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface tool-loading {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use spawn-types.{toolset-spec, toolset-state, admission-error};

    /// One toolset in the caller's session. A record, not a handle: the
    /// toolset lives in the workload's revision and the session, not in the
    /// caller's memory.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record loaded-toolset {
        /// The toolset's `toolset-spec.name`, unique among the caller's
        /// toolsets.
        name: string,
        /// The aliases its tools are offered under, in the toolset's own
        /// order. Model-facing names that carry no authority, stable for the
        /// session and never given to another toolset's tool, even after
        /// this one leaves; the same toolset name at a new digest takes them
        /// back. The host records which digest each alias denoted in the tool
        /// list sent with each model call, and refuses a call whose alias now
        /// denotes another digest (`unknown-tool`).
        aliases: list<string>,
        /// Where it stands.
        state: toolset-state,
        /// True when it is never set aside: the operator's or the person's
        /// own, or pinned by the person.
        pinned: bool,
        /// True for a toolset the workload declared (the operator's), false
        /// for one the caller or its model loaded.
        declared: bool,
    }

    /// What a load did.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record load-result {
        /// The toolset: `offered`, or `loading` when `wait` ran out first or
        /// when `next-run` is true.
        toolset: loaded-toolset,
        /// True when the toolset becomes callable only from the caller's
        /// next run (the next-run fallback).
        next-run: bool,
        /// Names of the toolsets this load set aside to stay within the
        /// function budget, now `ready`.
        set-aside: list<string>,
    }

    /// The caller's toolsets and its function budget.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record working-set {
        /// Every toolset bound or loading in the caller's session, declared
        /// ones first in declaration order, then loaded ones in load order.
        /// A toolset unbound at the component cap is not listed, but stays
        /// in the session ceiling, so loading it again needs no prompt.
        toolsets: list<loaded-toolset>,
        /// Functions offered to the model now, the host's own discovery
        /// tools included.
        offered-functions: u32,
        /// The model's function budget, where one applies.
        max-offered-functions: option<u32>,
    }

    /// Return the toolset if it is held already, refuse a name held by a
    /// different spec, and otherwise admit it and bind it. Resolves once it
    /// is callable or `wait` runs out.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    load: async func(spec: toolset-spec, wait: option<duration>) -> result<load-result, admission-error>;

    /// Set aside a toolset this caller loaded, by name: its definitions
    /// leave the model's tool list from the next `tools.list` on, and it
    /// becomes `ready`. Its component and approval stay, so loading it again
    /// needs no prompt and no rollout. Only a person removes a toolset from
    /// the session. A call in progress finishes. A toolset still `loading`
    /// answers `unavailable`; retry once it is `offered`. An unknown name is
    /// `invalid-request`; a pinned or declared toolset is `denied`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    unload: async func(name: string) -> result<_, admission-error>;

    /// The caller's toolsets and its function budget now.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    loaded: async func() -> working-set;
}
